Ladybird’s video sink now counts a frame as late only once the frame
after it is due, and leaves the container’s sample durations alone.
Chrome does the same: Its MP4StreamParser keeps each sample’s track-run
duration, and its VideoRendererAlgorithm::UpdateFrameStatistics() ends
each queued frame at the next frame’s timestamp. WebKit plays MP4
through AVFoundation, so its source shows nothing comparable.

Firefox instead rewrites the durations in its MP4 demuxer, so that each
sample ends at the next one’s composition time: Moof::Moof() in
MoofParser.cpp does that for each fragment, and create_sample_table() in
mp4parse does it for each sample table. Its TrackBuffersManager then
checks for a decode-time discontinuity against twice the longest frame
duration since the last keyframe, where the MSE spec says the last
frame’s duration. Rewriting the durations that way in Ladybird, with the
spec’s check left in place, split this file’s buffered range in five:
Some of its decode-time steps are 84ms, after frames shown for 42ms.

The issue’s file played directly, a plain MP4 remuxed from it with
“ffmpeg -c copy” (which keeps the uneven decode times), and the issue’s
file appended through MSE each play 4 seconds with no frames dropped in
Chrome 158, Firefox 151, and WebKit 26.5. Ladybird dropped 43, 45, and
37 of 96 before this change, and drops none after it.
